 ## Overview
-Binary Radiance Fields (BiRF) is a storage-efficient radiance field representation that constrains feature encoding parameters to binary values (+1 or -1) to achieve compact storage without post-processing compression [S1]. Unlike methods that compress pre-trained models or use real-valued embeddings, BiRF performs binarization during training, allowing it to outperform state-of-the-art efficient radiance field models with significantly lower storage overhead (e.g., < 0.5 MB) while maintaining high reconstruction quality [S1]. It builds on [Model Architecture and Encoding](13550-model-architecture-and-encoding.md) by replacing real-valued hash grid embeddings with binary ones and extending the architecture to a 2D-3D hybrid structure to mitigate hash collisions inherent in high-resolution 3D grids [S1].
+Binary Radiance Fields (BiRF) is a storage-efficient radiance field representation that constrains feature grid parameters to binary values (+1 or -1) to achieve compact storage without post-processing compression [S1]. Unlike methods that compress pre-trained models or use real-valued floating-point grids, BiRF performs binarization during training using a straight-through estimator, allowing it to maintain reconstruction quality with significantly reduced memory footprint [S1]. It builds on [Model Architecture and Encoding](13550-model-architecture-and-encoding.md) by replacing real-valued feature grids with binary ones and employing a 2D-3D hybrid multi-resolution architecture to mitigate hash collisions inherent in 3D grids [S1].
 
